# Kubernetes (Flux GitOps)

Every Kubernetes object in this cluster is declared here and reconciled by Flux
on push. There is no `kubectl apply` or `helm upgrade` in the normal flow: edit
YAML, commit, push.

## Layout

```
kubernetes/
├── clusters/{{ cluster_name }}/        # Flux entrypoint (`flux bootstrap --path` points here)
│   ├── flux-system/                    # Written by `flux bootstrap` — see its README
│   ├── infrastructure-sources.yaml     # stage 1  (no deps)
│   ├── infrastructure-crds.yaml        # stage 2  (dependsOn sources)
│   ├── infrastructure-controllers.yaml # stage 3  (dependsOn sources + crds)
│   ├── infrastructure-configs.yaml     # stage 4  (dependsOn controllers)
│   ├── infrastructure-observability.yaml  # stage 5 (dependsOn configs)
│   ├── apps.yaml                       # dependsOn configs — parallel to observability
│   ├── kustomization.yaml
│   └── tenants/                        # one file per external repo that deploys here
├── components/                         # shared Kustomize components (see below)
├── infrastructure/
│   ├── sources/        # HelmRepositories + cluster-versions + cluster-config
│   ├── crds/           # prometheus-operator CRDs, ahead of the controllers
│   ├── controllers/    # platform HelmReleases
│   ├── configs/        # CRs that need the controllers' CRDs
│   └── observability/  # metrics, logs, dashboards, alerting
└── apps/               # workloads, one directory per app
```

## Reconciliation order

`sources → crds → controllers → configs`, then `observability` and `apps` in
parallel. Two choices in that graph are deliberate and worth keeping:

- **`crds` is its own stage.** On a fresh cluster the Traefik chart renders a
  ServiceMonitor and hard-fails when the `monitoring.coreos.com` API is absent,
  and those CRDs otherwise arrive with the observability stack — two stages too
  late. Installing them first breaks the circle.
- **`apps` depends on `configs`, not on `observability`.** A failed metrics or
  logging upgrade must never freeze reconciliation of every workload.

## No site literals — the `cluster-config` ConfigMap

Nothing under `kubernetes/` contains a domain, an IP, a CIDR or a vault name.
Those live in exactly one file — `infrastructure/sources/cluster-config.yaml` —
and reach the manifests at reconcile time through
`postBuild.substituteFrom`. Chart and image versions work the same way through
the sibling `cluster-versions` ConfigMap. Every Flux Kustomization reads both.

Adding a literal to a manifest is a regression; add a key to `cluster-config`
instead. `tests/test_cluster_invariants.py` enforces this, along with "every
`${placeholder}` resolves to a key that exists" — important, because Flux and
`envsubst` both render an unknown placeholder as an **empty string** rather than
failing.

| Key | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `cluster_name` | Cluster identity; also the `clusters/<name>/` directory and the external-dns owner ID |
| `cluster_internal_domain` | Zone for LAN/tailnet-only hostnames |
| `cluster_external_domain` | Publicly resolvable zone |
| `cluster_node_label_domain` | Prefix for node role labels/taints (`<prefix>/ingress`, `<prefix>/gpu`) |
| `cluster_lan_cidr` | LAN range — ingress allowlists |
| `cluster_pod_cidr` / `cluster_service_cidr` | k3s CIDRs |
| `cluster_tailnet_cidr` | Tailscale CGNAT range |
| `cluster_api_vip` (reserved alias `cluster_k3s_api_vip`) | kube-apiserver VIP |
| `cluster_apiserver_egress_cidr` | What pod egress policies allow on 6443 |
| `cluster_etcd_endpoints` | etcd scrape targets for `kubeEtcd` — a **JSON flow sequence**, because Helm needs a real list where Flux substitutes text |
| `cluster_node_exporter_host_addresses` | EndpointSlice roster for the host node-exporters (`:9101`) — also a flow sequence |
| `cluster_zfs_exporter_addresses` | EndpointSlice roster for `zfs_exporter` (`:9134`) — flow sequence; ZFS storage backend only |
| `cluster_unbound_exporter_addresses` | EndpointSlice roster for `unbound_exporter` (`:9167`), one entry per resolver — also a flow sequence |
| `cluster_offsite_backup_probe_metric` | gates `OffsiteBackupStale`'s `absent()` arm; `up` until the offsite tier is enabled |
| `cluster_metallb_public_vip` / `cluster_metallb_internal_vip` | Ingress VIPs |
| `cluster_issuer` / `cluster_acme_email` | cert-manager ClusterIssuer name + ACME contact |
| `cluster_secret_store` | ClusterSecretStore every ExternalSecret references |
| `cluster_secrets_vault` | Vault the secret store reads |
| `cluster_git_host` / `cluster_runbook_base_url` | Git host; runbook link prefix for alerts |
| `cluster_nas_host` / `cluster_smtp_host` / `cluster_alert_email` | Host-side services the platform talks to |
| `cluster_upstream_dns_servers` | LAN resolvers in-cluster forwarders point at |
| `cluster_tailnet_dns_suffix` | MagicDNS suffix (`<tailnet>.ts.net`), tailnet module only |
| `cluster_node_exporter_job_regex` | Prometheus job name for host node-exporters |
| `cluster_ddns_records` | Records the DDNS CronJob maintains |

Values whose defaults are guesses are the first thing to check if something
resolves to the wrong place. Each of these is an answer, so correct it with
`copier update` (or in the ConfigMap for a one-off) rather than in the manifests
that substitute it.

## Shared components

| Component | Gives a namespace |
|---|---|
| `netpol-baseline` | `default-deny-ingress` |
| `netpol-egress-dns` | egress to cluster DNS |
| `netpol-egress-apiserver` | egress to the kube-apiserver on 6443 |
| `netpol-egress-public` | egress to public unicast only, never RFC1918/CGNAT/metadata |

Two things to know before using them:

1. Adding **any** egress policy makes egress default-deny for the pods it
   selects. A namespace that takes one egress component must take (or
   hand-write) every egress path its pods need.
2. `netpol-egress-public` selects all pods in the namespace. Where only some
   pods should reach the internet, patch its `podSelector` in the including
   kustomization — `controllers/external-secrets/` does exactly that.

The RFC-reserved `except:` list lives in that one component instead of being
copied into every policy. It is a standards constant, not this site's LAN.

## Node label contract

The Ansible k3s layer labels and taints nodes with the
`cluster_node_label_domain` prefix. The platform uses:

| Label / taint | Used by |
|---|---|
| `<prefix>/general: "true"` | Set on every agent; `apps/registry-cache` selects it (**required** — that pod stays Pending without it) |
| `<prefix>/nas: "true"` (+ matching `PreferNoSchedule` taint) | Storage-node affinity for the Prometheus/Loki/database volumes; also the DoesNotExist term that steers disposable workloads and CI job pods off it |
| `<prefix>/ingress: "true"` | Traefik node affinity (**preferred**, so an unlabelled cluster still schedules) |
| `<prefix>/gpu: nvidia` | NVIDIA device plugin node selector (**required** — that DaemonSet is for the GPU node only) |
| `<prefix>/cpu: modern` | CI job pods pin themselves here via `ci_cpu_selector`, and the runners only permit an override matching `^<prefix>/cpu=(modern\|legacy)$` — **required**, or those jobs are unschedulable. Set `legacy` on a node without modern CPU flags |
| `<prefix>/runner-class` | A **pod** label on CI job pods, with two fixed values: `infrastructure` and `shared`. kured refuses to reboot a node running an `infrastructure` job pod, and each runner's egress NetworkPolicy selects on it. It is the runner TIER, deliberately **not** the `ci_runner_tag` answer |
| `<prefix>/dns-api-token: "true"` | Namespace opt-in for the DNS-provider token fan-out |
| `<prefix>/cluster-secrets: "true"` | Namespace opt-in for the platform `ClusterSecretStore` — **required** on any namespace with an ExternalSecret (see below) |

Not every row is a node label: `runner-class` is set on CI job pods, and the
last two are set on Namespaces. They live in this table because they share the
prefix and the same opt-in-by-label mechanism — and because getting one of them
wrong fails silently rather than loudly.

## Scheduling priority

`infrastructure/sources/priorityclasses.yaml` defines two classes, and they live
in `sources` because the Priority admission plugin rejects a pod naming a class
that does not exist yet — `controllers` schedules such pods and reconciles first.

| Class | Value | Applied to |
|---|---|---|
| `platform` | 100000 | every platform HelmRelease, through that chart's own `priorityClassName` value path |
| `ci-jobs` | -10, `preemptionPolicy: Never` | CI job pods, through `priority_class_name` in both runner TOMLs |

`ci-jobs` is the load-bearing half: every unclassed pod sits at 0 and therefore
outranks a job pod without needing a class of its own, and `Never` means a burst
queues rather than evicting anything. `platform` is deliberately **not** set on
kured or metrics-server — both already carry a higher built-in class, so setting
it would be a downgrade.

## Backend seams

Only the backends the answers file offers are implemented; each is isolated so a
replacement is a bounded change.

- **Secrets** (`secrets_backend`): `configs/cluster-secret-store.yaml` (which is
  namespace-scoped — see below),
  `controllers/onepassword-connect/`, and the Connect egress patch in
  `controllers/external-secrets/kustomization.yaml`. Every ExternalSecret in the
  repo references `${cluster_secret_store}` and nothing else, so another
  provider means supplying a `ClusterSecretStore` of that name plus whatever
  credential it needs. Vault-item titles in `remoteRef.key` are backend-specific
  and would need remapping.
- **DNS** (`dns_backend`): the `solvers` block in `configs/cluster-issuer.yaml`,
  `provider` in `controllers/external-dns/release.yaml`, the token
  `ClusterExternalSecret` in `configs/shared-dns-secrets/`, and the DDNS CronJob.
  A new provider supplies a cert-manager DNS-01 solver, an external-dns provider
  name, and a Secret named `dns-api-token` with an `api-token` key.
- **Overlay VPN** (`vpn_tailscale`) and **GPU** (`gpu`) are additive modules:
  when off, their directories are not generated at all.

## Secret access is namespace-scoped

A `ClusterSecretStore` is cluster-scoped: without `spec.conditions`, **any**
namespace can name it in an ExternalSecret and read **every** item in the vault.
That is not theoretical here — a tenant namespace whose reconciliation
ServiceAccount holds the `admin` ClusterRole can create ExternalSecrets, so an
unconditioned store would hand every tenant the CI runner tokens, the identity
provider's keys and the SMTP credentials. RBAC inside the tenant's namespace
cannot re-impose the boundary; the store has to.

`configs/cluster-secret-store.yaml` therefore admits only namespaces labelled
`{{ internal_domain }}/cluster-secrets: "true"`. Namespace labels are set by
manifests in this repository, and the built-in `admin` ClusterRole grants only
`get`/`list`/`watch` on namespaces, so a tenant cannot label its way in.

- **Adding a platform module that needs a secret**: add the label to its
  `namespace.yaml`. Forgetting it is a loud failure — the ExternalSecret reports
  `no matching conditions` and `ExternalSecretSyncFailure` fires.
- **Onboarding a tenant**: do *not* add the label. Give the tenant its own
  `ClusterSecretStore`, its own provider token, and ideally its own vault. The
  pattern is in `clusters/{{ cluster_name }}/tenants/README.md`.

{% if git_backend == 'gitlab_selfhosted' %}## Before you register the CI runners

Read `apps/gitlab-runner-privileged/README.md` first, and take one rule from it
into the bring-up itself:

> **The privileged runner must be registered as a PROJECT runner locked to this
> repository. Never an instance runner; never a group runner over a group that
> contains untrusted projects.**

Tags are cooperative routing, not access control. That module's README carries
the reasoning, the residual risk `protected: false` accepts, and the
`GET /api/v4/runners/<id>` check to run after every registration.

{% endif %}## Bootstrap and day-2

`flux bootstrap` is a one-time step and is documented in
`clusters/{{ cluster_name }}/flux-system/README.md`; everything you must have in
place before it (secret backend credentials, DNS zone + API token, Git deploy
access) is in the template's [PRE-SETUP.md](https://git.ericsweiss.com/eric/weisssrv-cluster-template/-/blob/main/docs/PRE-SETUP.md).

Lint the whole tree before pushing — it builds every Kustomization, substitutes
from BOTH ConfigMaps, and runs kubeconform:

```bash
task flux:lint
```
